C = [
("h1", "3. Romans 14:1–12: Receive Him, and Do Not Judge"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 14:1–12 leaves meats and days to the believer’s own persuasion, and it forbids two things: the one who eats is not to despise the one who does not, and the one who does not eat is not to judge the one who does. Paul gives the ground in the first sentences, “for God hath received him,” and carries it to the Lord’s lordship over every believer."),
("sub", "3.1 The two parties as Paul describes them"),
("p", "The chapter opens with an instruction to the whole church: “Him that is weak in the faith receive ye, but not to doubtful disputations” (Romans 14:1). Paul then describes two believers by what they do. “For one believeth that he may eat all things: another, who is weak, eateth herbs” (Romans 14:2). Piper reads the verses a little more widely and observes that “those who are weak in faith don’t eat meat and don’t drink wine.” The word “wine” is not in verse 2. It is in verse 21, and the volume reads it there. In verse 2 the weak man eats herbs, and the strong man eats all things."),
("p", "Paul calls the first “weak” and gives him no other name. The volume therefore reports what the text says of him: that he is weak in the faith, that he eats herbs, that he esteems one day above another, and that his conscience can be hurt (Romans 14:14, 20, 21). It does not say more about him. Constable holds that “they appear to have been mainly Jewish Christians who refrained from certain foods,” and Baidoo argues that Romans 14 and 15 are “a targeted response to the postClaudian reintegration crisis in Rome.” Chapter 17 takes up that view. For the argument of this chapter it is enough that Paul does not name the weak as Jews or as Gentiles, and the instructions do not depend on who they were."),
("sub", "3.2 The two prohibitions"),
("p", "“Let not him that eateth despise him that eateth not; and let not him which eateth not judge him that eateth: for God hath received him” (Romans 14:3). Two sentences, two prohibitions, one reason. The strong are tempted to despise, and Piper says so: “Typically the strong will be tempted to ‘despise’ the weak—look down on them with a patronizing air.” The weak are tempted to judge. Paul speaks to both, and he gives both the same ground, that God has received the one whom the other would reject. The believer who judges or despises is refusing a man whom God has already received."),
("p", "Paul follows the ground with a question. “Who art thou that judgest another man’s servant? to his own master he standeth or falleth. Yea, he shall be holden up: for God is able to make him stand” (Romans 14:4). The brother is another’s servant, and the standing of a servant is his master’s concern. The second ground is therefore the ownership of the servant. The first is that God has received him. Neither ground says the weak man is right. Paul does not say that herbs are better or that all things are better. He says that the brother is not the judge’s servant."),
("sub", "3.3 Meats and days in one verdict"),
("p", "Paul puts days beside meats. “One man esteemeth one day above another: another esteemeth every day alike. Let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind” (Romans 14:5). Then he gives the verdict on both: “He that regardeth the day, regardeth it unto the Lord; and he that regardeth not the day, to the Lord he doth not regard it. He that eateth, eateth to the Lord, for he giveth God thanks; and he that eateth not, to the Lord he eateth not, and giveth God thanks” (Romans 14:6). The structure is the same for both matters: one does, one does not, each does it to the Lord, and Paul approves both."),
("p", "The volume argues from the verse that for the Body days are left to conscience. “Every day alike” is a position Paul allows. If a particular day were commanded to the Body, the man who esteemed every day alike would be disobeying, and Paul would not tell him to be fully persuaded in his own mind. The verse does not name the Sabbath, and the volume does not say that Paul has the Sabbath in view. It says what the verse says: the believer may keep a day or not keep it, and neither is to judge the other. Chapter 12 reads the same verdict in Colossians 2:16, 17, where “the sabbath days” are named. The Sabbath passages as a study of the day are in Study 78, which reports them as written, and the Law’s jurisdiction is in Study 59. This volume argues a conclusion that those studies leave open, and it states where its argument begins."),
("sub", "3.4 The lordship that carries it"),
("p", "The chapter turns from the quarrel to the reason beneath it. “For none of us liveth to himself, and no man dieth to himself. For whether we live, we live unto the Lord; and whether we die, we die unto the Lord: whether we live therefore, or die, we are the Lord’s” (Romans 14:7, 8). “For to this end Christ both died, and rose, and revived, that he might be Lord both of the dead and living” (Romans 14:9). The quarrel is over what a servant does; the answer is whose servant he is. The eater and the abstainer are the Lord’s alike, and the Lord’s lordship is the ground of the whole instruction."),
("sub", "3.5 The account each will give"),
("p", "“But why dost thou judge thy brother? or why dost thou set at nought thy brother? for we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ” (Romans 14:10). The verse puts the judge and the judged in the same place, before the same seat. Paul supports it with Scripture: “For it is written, As I live, saith the Lord, every knee shall bow to me, and every tongue shall confess to God” (Romans 14:11). He draws the conclusion: “So then every one of us shall give account of himself to God” (Romans 14:12). The account is each believer’s own. The volume does not take up the judgment seat of Christ here. It is read with the other courts in Prophecy & Mystery Volume 5, and the volume cites it only for the point Paul makes: that no brother is the judge of another’s account."),
("ctbl", "Romans 14:1–12 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "No, on eating and on days; he leaves each to the believer’s persuasion", "14:2, 5"),
    ("What limit?", "Receive the weak; the eater not despise; the abstainer not judge", "14:1, 3"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the brother whom God has received", "14:3, 4"),
    ("What ground?", "God hath received him; he is another’s servant; the Lord is Lord of all; each will give account of himself", "14:3, 4, 9, 12"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "Who the weak were; which day is meant; what the herbs were; what happens to the judge", "Chapters 3 and 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "3.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Meats and days are left to the believer’s own persuasion, and Paul approves the one who keeps and the one who does not.",
    "Two prohibitions are given, one to each party, on one ground: God hath received him.",
    "The lordship of Christ over all believers carries the instruction, and the account each gives is his own.",
    "Paul does not name the weak beyond what they do and what he calls them.",
  ]),
]
